WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:08.180
GILAD GOLAN: Hello,
and good morning,

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:12.220
or good evening, wherever or
whenever you're watching this.

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:15.100
Today, we're excited to
share with you a new product

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.810
announcement that has
been years in the making.

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:20.490
My name is Gilad Golan.

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:23.490
And I'm an Engineering
Director in GCP security,

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:26.220
working in
confidential computing.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:29.440
With me today is Nelly Porter,
the Lead Product Manager

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:31.440
for confidential computing.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:34.500
Later, we'll be joined by
Morgan Akers, Director of Data

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.680
Protection at JPMorgan Chase.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:40.690
So Nelly, let's start
from the beginning.

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:42.420
What is the data
protection problem

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:46.200
our customers are facing today?

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:47.670
NELLY PORTER: Thank you, Golan.

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:51.750
Really, really great to
have you here with me today.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:55.860
So data protection problem
is very important problem

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:56.790
for customers.

00:00:56.790 --> 00:01:01.680
And in all this time,
they had and understood

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.690
how to deal with this problem.

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:08.910
The data customers collected
was on their data centers.

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:11.600
And they know who
accessing this data.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:16.060
They had really good physical
perimeter across this data.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:21.270
They can control, not only
access, but also policy that

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:23.370
can be applied to this data.

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:25.920
And they knew that they
are responsible to protect

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:26.430
this data.

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:35.750
So no big news there everything
is familiar and expected.

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:38.600
Moving forward, with
exabytes of data

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:40.250
that our customers
collected, it's

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:44.040
becoming more and more difficult
to keep it on premises.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:46.100
So they're definitely
looking forward

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:49.790
to utilize cloud power
to process this data.

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:53.480
But then the situation
and security assurances

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:57.590
become a little more fuzzier
and more difficult to maintain,

00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:00.860
because sensitive data
is located not only

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:05.150
in one place that they have
full control over, on premises,

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:08.240
but also on different
public clouds.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:11.780
And what they understand
that they have now,

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:14.940
shared responsibility
for protecting this data.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:17.960
So not only they are in
control, but also they

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:20.870
need to share this
responsibility and control

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:22.740
with cloud providers.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:26.460
So the situation becoming
a little more complex.

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:29.870
And as a result of that,
there is multiple questions

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:32.270
and challenges
that our customers

00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:36.150
started to come up with
and trying to address.

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:39.710
And if I will summarize
what I hear loud and clear

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:42.830
from my customers,
there are concerns

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:50.270
around very basic topics like,
I have plenty of sensitive data,

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:54.170
I need to bring it to the
cloud, how I will protect it?

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:56.000
And it's not only about data.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:59.640
They develop significant
amount of IP.

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:02.420
And their application
and algorithms

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:04.050
have to be protected as well.

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:05.990
So how I will do that?

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:09.950
How will I stay compliant
with all regulations,

00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:13.510
and all other
rules, and auditors,

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:15.800
requirements that they
have to deal with?

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:18.680
Because it was a little
bit easier when I control,

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:23.620
end-to-end, all my protected
data and all my sensitive data.

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:26.720
But now, when I am
moving this to the cloud,

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:29.120
it's become more challenging.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:31.670
But it's not only
difficulties, it's

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:35.390
also ability to open a
new, very exciting use

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:39.090
cases that were not possible
before, for example,

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:42.980
the use case of collaboration.

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:45.710
How I will collaborate
with so many companies

00:03:45.710 --> 00:03:49.310
that I want to and
have to result in need

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:52.430
to share my sensitive
data and control over

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:54.210
that, with all of that?

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:57.110
Because some of them may
be even my competitors.

00:03:57.110 --> 00:04:02.390
And I am not fully trusting
them with my sensitive data.

00:04:02.390 --> 00:04:06.200
How would I be able to
process it together with them

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:10.520
and take advantage of the
results of this collaboration?

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:15.350
And also very, very important
question for all our customers,

00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:19.279
they are custodians of the
data with their own clients

00:04:19.279 --> 00:04:21.110
and their own users.

00:04:21.110 --> 00:04:25.160
And they want to do
their task and job right.

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:29.150
They want to protect
their data end-to-end.

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:32.870
So Gilad, with all
of those challenges,

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:39.060
is there any magic solution that
you would be able to offer us?

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:41.880
GILAD GOLAN: In fact,
Nelly, yes we do.

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.580
So we, at Google, believe
that the protection

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:47.050
of sensitive data is paramount.

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:49.290
And encryption is
a good mechanism

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:51.500
to accomplish this goal.

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:54.040
We supported, for years,
encryption in transit,

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:58.370
when our customers ingest the
data to bring it to the cloud.

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:00.510
We also supported
encryption at rest,

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:05.460
for data stored in the
cloud across all services.

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:09.240
Encrypted data in transit
and at rest is great.

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:10.950
But it is not sufficient.

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:14.590
Customers bring the data
to GCP for a reason.

00:05:14.590 --> 00:05:16.440
They want to index it.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:17.490
They want it queried.

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:19.680
They want to train
on it or infer

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:22.480
on the data for
their specific needs.

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:26.220
So to complete the data
protection lifecycle,

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:28.320
you would like to be
able to protect data

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:31.400
when it's being processed.

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:34.580
Confidential computing
is a new model

00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:36.590
that aims to close this gap.

00:05:36.590 --> 00:05:40.730
Confidential computing means
encrypted data in memory

00:05:40.730 --> 00:05:43.980
while processing it.

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:47.250
So with confidential
computing, customers

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:50.910
get encryption in use,
in addition to encryption

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:54.170
at rest and in transit.

00:05:54.170 --> 00:05:56.500
So Nelly, let's
get to the point.

00:05:56.500 --> 00:05:58.140
What are we announcing today?

00:06:01.128 --> 00:06:02.170
NELLY PORTER: Yes, Gilad.

00:06:02.170 --> 00:06:05.980
We will announce our
creation right away.

00:06:05.980 --> 00:06:09.800
But give me a few more
seconds to have some fun.

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:12.460
Let us dream a little bit.

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:15.790
What if with
whatever we're going

00:06:15.790 --> 00:06:21.670
to announce, literally right
after, this single, beautiful

00:06:21.670 --> 00:06:25.790
checkbox, you, my
dear customers,

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:30.920
you would be able to encrypt
all your workloads that you run

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:35.300
previously, and,
again, enjoyed so much,

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:39.680
and make them confidential,
simply transform them

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:43.400
into confidential environments?

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:48.230
What if you don't need to
pay significant performance

00:06:48.230 --> 00:06:52.700
and latency tax of doing
this transformation?

00:06:52.700 --> 00:06:56.420
And what if, for
some of you who have

00:06:56.420 --> 00:07:01.520
to provide audit reports to go
to your internal and external

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:05.220
auditors, you will be
able to do it very easily?

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:10.340
All of that about your
workloads, what if all of that

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:12.270
will become reality?

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:15.800
And it's exactly what we
want to announce today.

00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:18.920
After some really
serious work that we've

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:24.080
done, together with our
partners, AMD, we, today,

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:28.940
announcing confidential
VM on N2D series that

00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:35.180
powered by second generation
AMD EPYC processors in beta.

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:38.750
So Gilad, tell us what is that?

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:43.160
What is confidential
VMs all about?

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:45.870
GILAD GOLAN: So what
is confidential VM?

00:07:45.870 --> 00:07:50.010
Key thing to remember, It's
just like a regular VM.

00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:54.390
Customers can run any workload
that they usually run in VMs.

00:07:54.390 --> 00:07:57.240
And by clicking a
single checkbox,

00:07:57.240 --> 00:08:01.380
they will transform their
VMs into confidential VMs.

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:06.210
When they do that, all memory
pages off the confidential VM

00:08:06.210 --> 00:08:08.290
are encrypted.

00:08:08.290 --> 00:08:11.370
So you may rightly ask,
if they're encrypted,

00:08:11.370 --> 00:08:14.370
how does the CPU process them?

00:08:14.370 --> 00:08:17.480
So data is encrypted
in memory and is

00:08:17.480 --> 00:08:22.800
decrypted when it's fetched
from memory to the CPU chip.

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:25.190
And what about the
encryption keys?

00:08:25.190 --> 00:08:29.460
The keys are generated
in hardware on the CPU.

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:30.680
They're ephemeral.

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:34.970
They're not persisted anywhere
and cannot be extracted from

00:08:34.970 --> 00:08:39.169
the CPU chip by anyone,
including Google.

00:08:39.169 --> 00:08:44.630
Each VM memory is encrypted
by a unique key to that VM.

00:08:44.630 --> 00:08:48.410
And confidential VMs can
scale to meet your needs,

00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:55.470
up to 224 virtual CPUs and
896 gigabyte bandwidth.

00:08:55.470 --> 00:09:00.200
So finally, customers can
protect the data end-to-end,

00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:02.780
from data in transit,
to data at rest,

00:09:02.780 --> 00:09:06.800
and finally, to data in use.

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:07.920
NELLY PORTER: Yes, Gilad.

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:13.020
The way how you describe
the key protection

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:16.355
is very important
for confidential VM.

00:09:16.355 --> 00:09:20.800
The ability for
us to use hardware

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:23.720
generated keys which
it not extractable

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:25.960
is very, very nice property.

00:09:25.960 --> 00:09:28.830
It means nobody,
including Google or AMD,

00:09:28.830 --> 00:09:31.220
would be able to
get access to that.

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:34.060
Any other security
capability and [? strat ?]

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:37.790
[? medication ?] ideas that
you believe we will provide

00:09:37.790 --> 00:09:40.230
to our customers with
confidential VMs?

00:09:43.310 --> 00:09:46.140
GILAD GOLAN: Well,
as the name implies,

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:50.530
it simply adds confidentiality
while processing data.

00:09:50.530 --> 00:09:55.030
That means the hypervisor,
administrators, neighbors,

00:09:55.030 --> 00:09:58.660
and malicious devices
cannot snoop your data while

00:09:58.660 --> 00:10:01.030
it's in memory.

00:10:01.030 --> 00:10:03.400
As a multi-tenant
cloud provider,

00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:06.040
isolation has always
been a critical component

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:07.970
of our infrastructure.

00:10:07.970 --> 00:10:09.760
Now, with confidential
computing,

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:15.646
this isolation is getting
cryptographically reinforced.

00:10:15.646 --> 00:10:20.830
Nelly, can we see a demo
of confidential VMs?

00:10:20.830 --> 00:10:23.140
NELLY PORTER: I would love
to show the demo, Gilad.

00:10:23.140 --> 00:10:24.970
Thank you so much for asking.

00:10:24.970 --> 00:10:28.210
So the first demo that
we will have today

00:10:28.210 --> 00:10:32.950
is to demonstrate to our
customers, our viewers,

00:10:32.950 --> 00:10:36.520
how easy it is to
create confidential VM.

00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:41.500
And as we promised, initially,
it will be simple checkbox.

00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:43.000
And you will be
able to transition

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:47.860
your normal, usual VM
to confidential VM.

00:10:47.860 --> 00:10:51.380
Let me show you
how it can be done.

00:10:51.380 --> 00:10:57.380
So first, you come into
a usual GCP console.

00:10:57.380 --> 00:11:00.370
And you give the name
for confidential VM.

00:11:00.370 --> 00:11:03.570
And you will find a new
checkbox, literally checkbox,

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:07.880
the will explain that by
clicking this checkbox,

00:11:07.880 --> 00:11:10.730
your configuration will
be set for your VM.

00:11:10.730 --> 00:11:16.040
And all what you would need
to do is simply say Create.

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:17.120
And that's it.

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:21.710
And your confidential
VM is up and running.

00:11:21.710 --> 00:11:24.560
And to prove to you that
it's confidential VM,

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:27.650
let me as a Sage to this VM.

00:11:27.650 --> 00:11:31.880
And show it, to use it,
it's running on AMD server

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:34.940
with secure, encrypted
virtualization enabled.

00:11:34.940 --> 00:11:37.430
It, indeed, means
all your memory

00:11:37.430 --> 00:11:39.990
is going to be
encrypted, when it

00:11:39.990 --> 00:11:44.570
will process via code or data.

00:11:44.570 --> 00:11:49.740
The second demo that I want to
show you is slightly different.

00:11:49.740 --> 00:11:51.380
As you remember,
we were discussing

00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:57.630
performance latency and
performance and latency tax.

00:11:57.630 --> 00:12:00.560
So I want to demonstrate
you that, based

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:04.880
on what we've done so far and
old testing and benchmarks

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:08.500
we've done, we've seen that we
reached the envelope that we

00:12:08.500 --> 00:12:12.710
were putting on ourselves,
not more than 10%

00:12:12.710 --> 00:12:14.600
performance degradation.

00:12:14.600 --> 00:12:17.540
So we've seen, even, the
results on some of benchmarks

00:12:17.540 --> 00:12:19.930
that we've done
together with AMD teams

00:12:19.930 --> 00:12:23.510
that we are somewhere
around 2% to 6%,

00:12:23.510 --> 00:12:26.840
even better than we
actually put on ourselves.

00:12:26.840 --> 00:12:29.480
But every single
customer need to check.

00:12:29.480 --> 00:12:32.960
Please check your own workload
and your own performance

00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:35.540
benchmarks to see where you are.

00:12:35.540 --> 00:12:39.410
But let me show you
this quick demo.

00:12:39.410 --> 00:12:43.500
And the demo is showing
you very interesting race.

00:12:48.780 --> 00:12:54.790
We will have two VMs that will
start in the same-- almost

00:12:54.790 --> 00:12:56.110
the same time.

00:12:56.110 --> 00:12:59.470
Non-confidential VM will
come a little bit later

00:12:59.470 --> 00:13:01.450
than confidential VM.

00:13:01.450 --> 00:13:05.810
But to show you how these two
will perform, first of all,

00:13:05.810 --> 00:13:09.460
let's show you that on the
right side of your screen,

00:13:09.460 --> 00:13:13.800
you will have confidential,
left side is non-confidential.

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:16.670
And we will use SysBench,
the performance tool,

00:13:16.670 --> 00:13:19.330
to validate memory access.

00:13:19.330 --> 00:13:22.180
Memory access is
very important way

00:13:22.180 --> 00:13:26.230
for us to check how
confidential VM will perform

00:13:26.230 --> 00:13:29.040
compared non-confidential.

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:32.470
And because we're measuring
throughput, as you can see,

00:13:32.470 --> 00:13:34.300
comparable is great.

00:13:34.300 --> 00:13:36.290
And the bigger number is better.

00:13:36.290 --> 00:13:38.200
We've done memory read.

00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:40.060
Now, we will do memory write.

00:13:40.060 --> 00:13:44.000
And let's see how these
two VMs will perform.

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:47.110
And as you can see,
both of those VMs,

00:13:47.110 --> 00:13:51.350
confidential and non, are
performing very comparable.

00:13:51.350 --> 00:13:53.410
So we didn't see too
much degradation.

00:13:53.410 --> 00:13:57.790
But as I said, please validate
your performance results

00:13:57.790 --> 00:14:01.960
on your specific workloads,
and let us know where you are.

00:14:04.397 --> 00:14:06.480
GILAD GOLAN: Our announcement
of confidential VMs,

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:08.910
today, is just the first step.

00:14:08.910 --> 00:14:12.020
We believe that confidential
computing will, and should,

00:14:12.020 --> 00:14:16.290
be part of every
enterprise cloud point.

00:14:16.290 --> 00:14:19.890
Realizing this ambitious
goal requires collaboration

00:14:19.890 --> 00:14:23.310
across the entire industry,
both cloud providers,

00:14:23.310 --> 00:14:25.620
hardware manufacturers,
and software vendors

00:14:25.620 --> 00:14:28.440
working together
to define standards

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:30.840
and advanced technology.

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:32.910
This is why Google
was among the founding

00:14:32.910 --> 00:14:36.900
members of the Confidential
Computing Consortium

00:14:36.900 --> 00:14:40.470
operating under the umbrella
of the Linux Foundation.

00:14:40.470 --> 00:14:45.660
Together with other key players,
like Microsoft, Intel, AMD,

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:50.110
ARM, Alibaba, IBM,
and many others,

00:14:50.110 --> 00:14:55.250
we are working together to
advance confidential computing.

00:14:55.250 --> 00:14:59.870
And now I'd like to hear
the perspective of a cloud

00:14:59.870 --> 00:15:01.180
customer.

00:15:01.180 --> 00:15:06.133
I'd like introduce Morgan
from JPMorgan Chase.

00:15:06.133 --> 00:15:11.910
NELLY PORTER: Gilad, I want to
add one more interesting tip.

00:15:11.910 --> 00:15:15.780
Morgan Akers that will
join us right away

00:15:15.780 --> 00:15:19.450
is also part of Confidential
Computing Consortium.

00:15:19.450 --> 00:15:22.260
He is representing
customer voice

00:15:22.260 --> 00:15:26.400
in this multi-industry
consortium.

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:30.690
So it'll be very interesting
to hear from Morgan

00:15:30.690 --> 00:15:35.890
how he was doing and what is
learning he had from working

00:15:35.890 --> 00:15:39.060
with our original pre-build.

00:15:39.060 --> 00:15:41.462
Morgan, now it's you.

00:15:41.462 --> 00:15:43.170
MORGAN AKERS: Thank
you very much, Nelly.

00:15:43.170 --> 00:15:44.670
I appreciate the
introduction, here.

00:15:44.670 --> 00:15:47.700
My name is Morgan Akers,
as Nelly mentioned.

00:15:47.700 --> 00:15:50.310
I am a director in the
cybersecurity space

00:15:50.310 --> 00:15:53.430
inside of JPMC,
and specifically,

00:15:53.430 --> 00:15:57.460
in the Data Management and
Protection organization.

00:15:57.460 --> 00:16:00.720
If you look at JPMC,
it's one of the oldest

00:16:00.720 --> 00:16:03.120
financial institutions
in the world.

00:16:03.120 --> 00:16:06.990
They work across the geographies
into a global company,

00:16:06.990 --> 00:16:09.420
multiple financial
sectors, customers

00:16:09.420 --> 00:16:12.540
ranging from consumer banking,
to corporate services,

00:16:12.540 --> 00:16:15.240
to government services, to
asset wealth management.

00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:18.960
So really, they run the gamut
in terms of financial sectors.

00:16:18.960 --> 00:16:23.010
Additionally, we have a wide
swath of both employees,

00:16:23.010 --> 00:16:25.810
in terms of different types of
roles, as well as customers.

00:16:25.810 --> 00:16:29.010
And so when I think about
managing and protecting

00:16:29.010 --> 00:16:31.590
our data as a mission for data
management and protection,

00:16:31.590 --> 00:16:35.990
it's really, how do we enable
data to be managed correctly

00:16:35.990 --> 00:16:39.180
and succinctly across
the firm seamlessly

00:16:39.180 --> 00:16:40.590
and without expertise.

00:16:40.590 --> 00:16:44.130
What I mean by this is
as a user of the data,

00:16:44.130 --> 00:16:46.260
you should not have
to know, explicitly,

00:16:46.260 --> 00:16:47.350
what to do with this data.

00:16:47.350 --> 00:16:49.680
The system should
manage it for you.

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:51.210
You shouldn't have
to be expected

00:16:51.210 --> 00:16:53.943
to understand that this
dataset is this sensitivity,

00:16:53.943 --> 00:16:55.860
and I know how to actually
manage that policy.

00:16:55.860 --> 00:16:57.510
You shouldn't rely
on tribal knowledge.

00:16:57.510 --> 00:16:59.830
This should be something
the system handles for you.

00:16:59.830 --> 00:17:02.190
And when you think about that
seamless and frictionless

00:17:02.190 --> 00:17:04.319
experience of
managing data, that

00:17:04.319 --> 00:17:06.230
is, really, the pillar
that we build on

00:17:06.230 --> 00:17:07.980
in terms of maintaining
the chain of trust

00:17:07.980 --> 00:17:10.418
between our users,
and our services,

00:17:10.418 --> 00:17:11.460
and how to use that data.

00:17:14.930 --> 00:17:16.640
Now, data management
and protection

00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:19.819
is, really, effectively,
three different pillars,

00:17:19.819 --> 00:17:22.859
in terms of how we actually
think about our architecture.

00:17:22.859 --> 00:17:24.720
The first one is classification.

00:17:24.720 --> 00:17:28.700
Classification is, really, the
simplest piece of understanding

00:17:28.700 --> 00:17:32.210
what kind of data you're
dealing with, who generated it,

00:17:32.210 --> 00:17:34.400
what kind of data it
is, sensitivity level,

00:17:34.400 --> 00:17:35.660
is it business impacting?

00:17:35.660 --> 00:17:37.550
Is it critical data?

00:17:37.550 --> 00:17:39.560
As well as, what
type of data is it?

00:17:39.560 --> 00:17:42.080
Is it programmatically
generated by a service?

00:17:42.080 --> 00:17:44.870
Is it hand-authored, like
an email, or something

00:17:44.870 --> 00:17:46.160
that's a Word document?

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:47.530
And how is it used?

00:17:47.530 --> 00:17:49.290
And where is it coming from?

00:17:49.290 --> 00:17:51.770
So with that classification
of that data,

00:17:51.770 --> 00:17:54.440
with as fine grain of
classification as we can get,

00:17:54.440 --> 00:17:57.320
we move to how we do our
policy decision engines.

00:17:57.320 --> 00:18:00.830
Now, for every piece of data
based on the classification,

00:18:00.830 --> 00:18:04.790
we want to create a policy that
says, given this type of data

00:18:04.790 --> 00:18:06.860
and who is using it,
what I should do,

00:18:06.860 --> 00:18:08.420
that's the decision aspect.

00:18:08.420 --> 00:18:11.480
And we really want to do it from
both a build-time perspective

00:18:11.480 --> 00:18:12.440
as well as a runtime.

00:18:12.440 --> 00:18:15.170
And what I mean by that
is that it's not just

00:18:15.170 --> 00:18:17.990
what I'm programmatically
building my system in terms

00:18:17.990 --> 00:18:21.860
of managing the data as I
build my developer toolkits

00:18:21.860 --> 00:18:25.310
and my systems across the
financial institution, but also

00:18:25.310 --> 00:18:27.490
runtime, for that
real-time decision process,

00:18:27.490 --> 00:18:29.240
where an influx of data comes.

00:18:29.240 --> 00:18:31.490
You need to be able to make
those decisions on the fly

00:18:31.490 --> 00:18:33.260
to apply that policy.

00:18:33.260 --> 00:18:36.470
Once that policy is
applied and a decision

00:18:36.470 --> 00:18:39.740
is made, you move to data
healing and enforcement, which

00:18:39.740 --> 00:18:42.440
is, essentially,
what do I do now?

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:44.530
Is the data encrypted?

00:18:44.530 --> 00:18:47.180
Do you make it readable by that
individual or that service?

00:18:47.180 --> 00:18:49.880
Is it editable by that
reader or service?

00:18:49.880 --> 00:18:51.020
Can you use a token?

00:18:51.020 --> 00:18:52.570
Do you need to decrypt it?

00:18:52.570 --> 00:18:55.070
Those are the aspects of what
I would consider data handling

00:18:55.070 --> 00:18:56.030
and enforcement.

00:18:56.030 --> 00:18:59.060
So these three things,
together, form the backbone

00:18:59.060 --> 00:19:04.300
of what we think about for
data management and protection.

00:19:04.300 --> 00:19:06.630
Now, when we look at
Confidential Compute

00:19:06.630 --> 00:19:11.010
as an environment, as what
I would consider an area

00:19:11.010 --> 00:19:14.820
to grow and think
about strategically,

00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:16.710
it's still somewhat
in its infancy.

00:19:16.710 --> 00:19:19.140
So our approach to
confidential computing

00:19:19.140 --> 00:19:23.092
is what do we think we can use
it for that would inform us

00:19:23.092 --> 00:19:25.050
of a decision, and how
do we, then, incorporate

00:19:25.050 --> 00:19:26.380
in our strategy?

00:19:26.380 --> 00:19:28.715
So when we're looking at
our Confidential Compute

00:19:28.715 --> 00:19:30.840
environment, one of the
things that we came up with

00:19:30.840 --> 00:19:34.960
was there is typical models
for user authentication.

00:19:34.960 --> 00:19:37.770
But it's not as strong in terms
of a standard model for what

00:19:37.770 --> 00:19:40.290
I would call instance
or host identity

00:19:40.290 --> 00:19:42.540
models of identification,
especially when

00:19:42.540 --> 00:19:44.957
you're dealing with multiple
routers and multiple systems.

00:19:44.957 --> 00:19:46.748
And we thought this
was ideal for something

00:19:46.748 --> 00:19:48.870
that maybe a Confidential
Compute could solve for.

00:19:48.870 --> 00:19:52.500
So what we'd think about is
user and instance identity

00:19:52.500 --> 00:19:56.820
is foundational to us securing
sensitive data, who you are,

00:19:56.820 --> 00:19:59.670
who you're working on behalf
of, and where it's coming from

00:19:59.670 --> 00:20:01.830
is part of that
classification aspect,

00:20:01.830 --> 00:20:03.450
as well as where should go to.

00:20:03.450 --> 00:20:06.720
If we build what I call a chain
of trust inside of Confidential

00:20:06.720 --> 00:20:09.470
Compute, we can do,
effectively, this mapping

00:20:09.470 --> 00:20:12.750
that says, all right, service
A can talk to service B.

00:20:12.750 --> 00:20:16.260
And the two together will build
that informational system that

00:20:16.260 --> 00:20:18.330
understands what we should do.

00:20:18.330 --> 00:20:21.640
Now, when I look at
how we manage this,

00:20:21.640 --> 00:20:23.610
there is the
customer managed key

00:20:23.610 --> 00:20:25.770
that the bank informs us of.

00:20:25.770 --> 00:20:28.020
And that, actually,
is what we use

00:20:28.020 --> 00:20:30.900
to create the authentication
request, who does what,

00:20:30.900 --> 00:20:33.000
and how they actually
talk to the systems.

00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:35.160
And that Confidential
Compute system

00:20:35.160 --> 00:20:37.360
is where we're
managing that mapping.

00:20:37.360 --> 00:20:39.690
So effectively, that
managing of the mapping

00:20:39.690 --> 00:20:42.270
is in the node of
Confidential Compute.

00:20:42.270 --> 00:20:45.240
It's a user, the
application, or the service,

00:20:45.240 --> 00:20:48.070
and the resource that's running
the application service.

00:20:48.070 --> 00:20:50.890
And we can use it to both
audit the chains that we make,

00:20:50.890 --> 00:20:53.003
as well as the union
of whether you should

00:20:53.003 --> 00:20:54.170
grant that handshake or not.

00:20:58.400 --> 00:21:00.740
Now, when we think
about this, this

00:21:00.740 --> 00:21:03.720
is a general pattern
of discovery.

00:21:03.720 --> 00:21:06.680
And you can see by the
big boxes and arrows

00:21:06.680 --> 00:21:09.730
that this is very much
a high level pattern.

00:21:09.730 --> 00:21:12.540
And it doesn't describe anything
in intimate detail here.

00:21:12.540 --> 00:21:14.510
But what we really were
trying to figure out

00:21:14.510 --> 00:21:17.150
was if I create this
service to instance mapping

00:21:17.150 --> 00:21:19.440
in a Confidential Compute,
how would that work?

00:21:19.440 --> 00:21:23.120
Well, general principles are
that the service A and service

00:21:23.120 --> 00:21:25.470
B want to actually
talk to each other.

00:21:25.470 --> 00:21:29.270
And so service A would actually
go to our Confidential Compute

00:21:29.270 --> 00:21:32.890
system and say, all right,
give me a handshake or a token

00:21:32.890 --> 00:21:35.170
and authenticate that
I am this service,

00:21:35.170 --> 00:21:36.920
and I'm an implementation
of this service,

00:21:36.920 --> 00:21:41.030
and I want to talk to service
B. The covetous computing

00:21:41.030 --> 00:21:43.700
node would actually take the
mapping and say, OK, service A,

00:21:43.700 --> 00:21:46.130
you are actually cleared
to talk to service B.

00:21:46.130 --> 00:21:50.383
And here's a one time token to
use, with a time limit on it.

00:21:50.383 --> 00:21:51.800
So we effectively
put a TTL on it.

00:21:51.800 --> 00:21:53.568
So it's very short lived.

00:21:53.568 --> 00:21:55.526
Give that back to service
A. Service A presents

00:21:55.526 --> 00:21:59.090
it to service B. Service B then
presents it back to our trusted

00:21:59.090 --> 00:22:02.000
system in our confidential
computing system,

00:22:02.000 --> 00:22:04.010
and it verified that
the handshake is real.

00:22:04.010 --> 00:22:06.260
And it let the two
decrypt the request.

00:22:06.260 --> 00:22:08.060
And it's authenticated.

00:22:08.060 --> 00:22:10.820
What we've done is we've
moved the authentication

00:22:10.820 --> 00:22:13.070
of this information
about the services

00:22:13.070 --> 00:22:16.310
away from being done inside
of service A or service

00:22:16.310 --> 00:22:19.310
B. It's actually being
done in a third system,

00:22:19.310 --> 00:22:21.658
and that's Confidential
Compute, that mappings done.

00:22:21.658 --> 00:22:23.450
And service A and
service B can concentrate

00:22:23.450 --> 00:22:25.450
on what they're supposed
to be good at, which is

00:22:25.450 --> 00:22:26.930
their processing information.

00:22:26.930 --> 00:22:29.090
That reduces the
overall threat matrix

00:22:29.090 --> 00:22:31.440
in terms of what service
A and service B can do,

00:22:31.440 --> 00:22:33.980
because the mapping and that
verification of credentials

00:22:33.980 --> 00:22:37.100
is being done inside a
Confidential Compute.

00:22:37.100 --> 00:22:39.020
And as a pattern, this
is interesting to us,

00:22:39.020 --> 00:22:41.270
because what it
does is it leverages

00:22:41.270 --> 00:22:43.520
a lot of the key
infrastructures with services.

00:22:43.520 --> 00:22:45.920
But also it takes
advantage of the idea

00:22:45.920 --> 00:22:48.840
that Confidential Compute is a
protected and close environment

00:22:48.840 --> 00:22:50.600
that can manage this
mapping securely.

00:22:53.440 --> 00:22:56.350
So as I mentioned,
that previous pattern,

00:22:56.350 --> 00:22:58.040
that's just a general
use case that we

00:22:58.040 --> 00:23:02.745
were thinking of an opportunity
of Confidential Compute.

00:23:02.745 --> 00:23:04.120
There's additional
things that we

00:23:04.120 --> 00:23:06.490
think could be used
for in the future.

00:23:06.490 --> 00:23:08.470
Secret storage is one
of the key ones, right.

00:23:08.470 --> 00:23:12.670
Managing keys, securing
those keys in its system,

00:23:12.670 --> 00:23:17.110
it's just a generally natural
fit for confidential computing.

00:23:17.110 --> 00:23:19.420
The second one is a trust
and integrity chain.

00:23:19.420 --> 00:23:21.790
The previous example
demonstrated a chain of trust

00:23:21.790 --> 00:23:23.950
with service and
instance mapping.

00:23:23.950 --> 00:23:25.510
But that can
actually be expanded

00:23:25.510 --> 00:23:30.035
to integrity chains of who
changes the data over time

00:23:30.035 --> 00:23:31.660
and what that
information has happened.

00:23:31.660 --> 00:23:34.390
Or maybe you have a
trusted vendor relationship

00:23:34.390 --> 00:23:35.413
across a dataset.

00:23:35.413 --> 00:23:37.330
And you want to understand
where that went to.

00:23:37.330 --> 00:23:39.205
That chain and that
management could actually

00:23:39.205 --> 00:23:42.160
be stored in terms of
Confidential Compute.

00:23:42.160 --> 00:23:43.720
Another one is a
machine identity.

00:23:43.720 --> 00:23:46.330
Now, we were talking
about instance identity

00:23:46.330 --> 00:23:47.178
and authentication.

00:23:47.178 --> 00:23:48.970
But when I say machine
identity is actually

00:23:48.970 --> 00:23:51.982
putting the materials
on the box themselves.

00:23:51.982 --> 00:23:53.690
Typically, that's done
with certificates.

00:23:53.690 --> 00:23:56.990
But what if we actually used
Confidential Compute to manage

00:23:56.990 --> 00:23:59.778
the certificates and the
resources of those systems,

00:23:59.778 --> 00:24:01.570
and there is, effectively,
checking points.

00:24:01.570 --> 00:24:04.300
That confidential
computing system

00:24:04.300 --> 00:24:06.760
could be the one that manages
certificates and identities

00:24:06.760 --> 00:24:08.230
for those.

00:24:08.230 --> 00:24:13.090
Post-processing audit/log data,
now, audit data, log data,

00:24:13.090 --> 00:24:16.450
generally, they're only as good
as the data that's in them.

00:24:16.450 --> 00:24:19.078
And you typically need them to
be readable and parsable to be

00:24:19.078 --> 00:24:20.620
able to make the
most of it, creating

00:24:20.620 --> 00:24:23.170
those events, understanding
what happened to the chain.

00:24:23.170 --> 00:24:25.870
But with that, you
have clear text data

00:24:25.870 --> 00:24:27.790
that could pose a
danger and a threat.

00:24:27.790 --> 00:24:30.490
If we're able to put that
data in clear, effectively,

00:24:30.490 --> 00:24:33.970
confidential computing systems,
they can post-process that

00:24:33.970 --> 00:24:35.500
data, get the data--

00:24:35.500 --> 00:24:37.900
maybe trigger those
events in a secure way,

00:24:37.900 --> 00:24:40.030
and then dispose of
that clear text data.

00:24:40.030 --> 00:24:42.130
And actually, you're
focusing all the processing

00:24:42.130 --> 00:24:43.900
in Confidential Compute.

00:24:43.900 --> 00:24:47.230
And then lastly, is what we
call a trusted enclave for data

00:24:47.230 --> 00:24:47.740
transforms.

00:24:47.740 --> 00:24:51.100
But really, it's just a secure
environment for aggregating

00:24:51.100 --> 00:24:53.410
data, multiple
datasets that you want

00:24:53.410 --> 00:24:55.750
to put together and
generate queries,

00:24:55.750 --> 00:24:58.480
searchable indexes,
and something as simple

00:24:58.480 --> 00:24:59.990
as even machine learning.

00:24:59.990 --> 00:25:02.770
If you want to apply machine
learning or other type

00:25:02.770 --> 00:25:07.240
of algorithms to data,
typically, those algorithms

00:25:07.240 --> 00:25:10.240
need to operate on the
data in their natural form.

00:25:10.240 --> 00:25:13.690
If you can actually put that
into a confidential computing

00:25:13.690 --> 00:25:15.805
environment that can
actually do that processing,

00:25:15.805 --> 00:25:17.930
and you just worry about
the output coming through,

00:25:17.930 --> 00:25:19.630
and then dispose
of the data that's

00:25:19.630 --> 00:25:23.060
inside that confidential
computing node.

00:25:23.060 --> 00:25:25.550
NELLY PORTER: Thank you,
everyone for watching

00:25:25.550 --> 00:25:29.240
our show and our announcement.

00:25:29.240 --> 00:25:32.240
GILAD GOLAN: So to summarize,
confidential computing

00:25:32.240 --> 00:25:35.900
is an industry-wide effort
critical for securing

00:25:35.900 --> 00:25:38.470
sensitive workloads.

00:25:38.470 --> 00:25:41.770
We're focusing on hardware-based
encryption for data in use,

00:25:41.770 --> 00:25:46.530
with the goal of enabling it
for any workload of any size.

00:25:46.530 --> 00:25:48.690
Our goal is to
offer our customers

00:25:48.690 --> 00:25:50.510
a no-compromise solution.

00:25:50.510 --> 00:25:54.540
The security you need without
giving up on usability,

00:25:54.540 --> 00:25:57.520
performance, and scale.

00:25:57.520 --> 00:26:01.620
Thank you all for joining us
today for this presentation.

